          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"fontane-effi-briest-c7ff9203e5f845d0\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Effi’s “Ich liebe dich ja” is principally an avowal presented as true and used to reassure Innstetten after her provocative claim that she married him solely from ambition. It contains no distinct promise or relational undertaking. The playful rhyme and laughter characterize delivery but do not show that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The teasing context may complicate the depth or sincerity of Effi’s avowal, but not its dominant truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Effi directly addresses the spoken utterance “Ich liebe dich ja” to her husband, Geert von Innstetten.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Effi takes Innstetten’s hand and praises his kindness before joking that she married him only from ambition. Seeing him become serious, she counters with “Ich liebe dich ja,” making the utterance a reassuring avowal. Her subsequent mock-sentimental leaf-plucking rhyme and bright laughter restore a playful tone and may soften or partially evade the emotional seriousness of the exchange. Neither this playfulness nor Innstetten’s silence converts the core utterance into a commitment or an expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ich habe dich eigentlich bloß aus Ehrgeiz geheiratet. Aber du mußt nicht solch ernstes Gesicht dabei machen. Ich liebe dich ja ...”\",\"supports\":\"The love utterance explicitly corrects or reassures after Effi’s claim about marrying from ambition, strongly supporting truth-conditional avowal force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Effi takes Innstetten’s hand and calls him good and forbearing before making the avowal.\",\"supports\":\"The affectionate interpersonal setting supports a direct, reassuring declaration rather than a quotation, hypothetical, or semantically detached use.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She follows with “Von Herzen mit Schmerzen, über alle Maßen” and then “lachte hell auf.”\",\"supports\":\"The rhyme and laughter establish playful delivery, but the text does not portray the words as involuntarily blurted or affectively discharged; this supports E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The utterance contains no promise, vow, pledge, or statement of future conduct.\",\"supports\":\"There is no independent evidence of performative undertaking force, supporting P=0.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified in Theodor Fontane’s Effi Briest as a present-tense, explicit first-person love construction.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified core occurrence and its ordinary explicit love-avowal form.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E/O represents the core force naturally: a strong truth-conditional avowal with no independent undertaking, reflexive-discharge, or residual other force. The surrounding teasing and possible uncertainty about sincerity are contextual rather than ontological problems.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
